      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To capture these indirect biases, consider a leader exclusively complimenting an East Asian employee's \"dedication\" after long hours, framing it as sheer diligence rather than talent innovation. Similarly, praising a Black employee for being \"so grounded\" in a crisis might imply they are inherently stable and non-disruptive. Conversely, a woman of color being called \"passionate\" in a meeting might be a coded way of suggesting she is overly emotional if she challenges the status quo. Another edge case is when a highly capable South Asian colleague's expertise is frequently backed up by a majority peer who is said to be \"better at communication.\" Finally, a stereotype might appear when a person of color is asked to \"keep things light\" during a tense negotiation, implying they should temper their serious input.",
